  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1245 AM CDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nThe dangerous heatwave will continue through the remainder of the \nweek with a stout ridge of high pressure in control over the lower \nOhio Valley. A Heat Advisory remains in effect for the entire CWA \nthrough Friday for heat index values ranging from 100-108, with most \nlocations around 103-106 on average. Compared to Monday, model \nsoundings support an even deeper dry adiabatic layer upwards of 700 \nmb, where the NAEFS indicates 30-year return intervals for \ntemperatures that fall above the 99th percentile. Given more \nsufficient compression at the sfc and the potential to mix down \nslightly lower dewpoints in the upper 60s during peak diurnal \nheating, daily highs ranging from the upper 90s to locally 100 \ndegrees are probable. Should afternoon dewpoints remain more \nelevated in the lower 70s, a upgrade to an Extreme Heat Warning may \nneed to be considered as this forecast package has been blended \ntowards CONS guidance. For now, locations exceeding a daily heat \nindex of 105 is still too sporadic until the latter half of the \nweek, but there will be exceptions locally. Several record warm \novernight low temperatures are also possible this week in the mid \n70s. \n\nAs for the potential for rain chances that will bring some relief, \nthe ECMWF has trended more towards the GFS with the bulk of the \nchances holding off until late Saturday into Saturday night. The \ndelay of the ridge breaking down means a 70-90% chance on the LREF \nfor needing to extend the heat headlines into Saturday. Shower and \nthunderstorm chances will also be pretty hit or miss, with a 20-30% \nchance of rainfall amounts exceeding a tenth of an inch on the \nGEFS/EPS. Depending on how quickly a cold front moves through on \nSunday that will bring an end to the heatwave, rain chances may \nlinger.",
